;; confidence-range — a claim's confidence must lie in [0.0, 1.0]
;; (REQ-KG-003 / REQ-KG-012).
;;
;; Booklogic port of the tbf:confidence range constraint in assets/shapes.ttl:
;;   sh:path tbf:confidence ; sh:minInclusive 0.0 ; sh:maxInclusive 1.0 .
;;
;; This rule covers the sh:maxInclusive arm (confidence > 1.0) — the case the
;; C0.1 violating fixture injects (inj-bad-confidence has confidence 1.5, and
;; the C0.2 golden carries "Value is not <= ...1.0..."). The literal 1.0 stays
;; UNQUOTED so it compares against the typed Float column; the compiler guards
;; the comparison with !is_null so a null-confidence claim is dropped (its
;; missing-value concern belongs to the minCount shape, not the range shape).
;;
;; The sh:minInclusive 0.0 arm (confidence < 0.0) is a SECOND rule
;; (confidence-range-low.edn) — the compiler has no OR/disjunction within one
;; rule, so each inequality is its own constraint. Both arms carry the SAME
;; authored :message and the SAME :path so the canonical message-remap keyed on
;; #confidence stays unambiguous (C-1: both engines now agree on the full
;; [0.0, 1.0] range).
;;
;; MESSAGE NOTE: pyshacl auto-generates the range message
;; ("Value is not <= Literal(\"1.0\", ...)") / ("Value is not >= ...0.0..."); the
;; rdflib path remaps both, keyed on the #confidence path, to the authored
;; message below.
(defconstraint :confidence-range
  :message "Claim confidence must be in [0.0, 1.0]."
  :path "https://example.org/book-knowledge#confidence"
  :component "http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#MaxInclusiveConstraintComponent"
  :where [[?c :claim/id ?focus]
          [?c :claim/confidence ?conf]]
  :filter [[> ?conf 1.0]])
